The GraphQL endpoint you need is hosted in Scandiweb’s official GitHub repository for their junior developer assignment.[github]
Repository and purpose
•	Repository: scandiweb/junior-react-endpoint[github]
•	Purpose: Provides a local GraphQL API that you’ll query from your “minimalistic storefront” React app during the assessment.[github]
•	Important rule: You are not supposed to modify this endpoint code or submit it as part of your assessment.[github]
How to start the endpoint
Clone the repo and run:
bash
# 1. Install dependencies
yarn install

# 2. Build the project
yarn build

# 3. Start the GraphQL server
yarn start
After yarn start, the server usually runs on http://localhost:8080 (check the console output; the exact port is printed there).[github]
Using the endpoint in your app
In your React project, configure Apollo Client (or another GraphQL client) to point to the local URL, e.g.:
js
import { ApolloClient, InMemoryCache, gql } from '@apollo/client';

const client = new ApolloClient({
  uri: 'http://localhost:8080', // or the URL shown in your terminal
  cache: new InMemoryCache(),
});
Then you can run queries like (example based on typical Scandiweb tasks):
js
const GET_PRODUCTS = gql`
  query {
    products {
      id
      name
      price
      image
    }
  }
`;
Exact schema (available queries/mutations) is defined in the endpoint repo; you can inspect the source or use a GraphQL playground (if enabled) at http://localhost:8080 once the server is running.[github]
